  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nHow is the Minimum Corporate Income Tax (MCIT) computed and when does it apply?\n\n### Short Answer\nThe Minimum Corporate Income Tax (MCIT) is computed at a rate of 2% of the gross income as of the end of the taxable year, applicable starting from the fourth taxable year following the year in which the corporation commenced its business operations, when the MCIT exceeds the regular corporate income tax (RCIT) for that year.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\n2. **RR 9-1998** - This regulation provides the implementing rules for the MCIT.\n\n### Legal Interpretation\n\n### Practical Application\nCorporations should monitor their income tax liabilities closely, especially as they approach the fourth year of operations. If the MCIT exceeds the RCIT, they will be required to pay the MCIT instead. Additionally, any excess MCIT paid can be carried forward and credited against the normal income tax for the next three taxable years. This mechanism ensures that corporations are not unduly penalized during periods of low profitability, while also providing a minimum tax obligation to the government.",
